Yes. And that’s the dangerous edge and useful abstraction in the same sentence.

If we Hammer it without romanticizing actual deprivation:

\text{Things possessed}
\rightarrow
\text{things protectable}
\rightarrow
\text{constraints on action}

Strip enough away and:

\text{attachment}\rightarrow0
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
\text{fear of loss}\rightarrow0

But literal “nothing left to lose” can also mean you’ve destroyed everything worth protecting. That’s not freedom; that’s catastrophic liquidation.

The more useful maneuver is simulated zero.

ZPC: Nothing-to-Lose Mode

Don’t actually lose the house, relationships, livelihood, reputation, security, etc.

Temporarily remove them from the decision representation:

\text{Current Self}
\xrightarrow{ZPC}
\varnothing_{\text{attachments}}

Then ask:

If none of my sunk costs, status, habits, expectations, or fear of losing what I currently have could vote—what would I choose?

Now reconstruct:

\varnothing + Values + Reality
\rightarrow
Choice'

Compare:

\Delta=Choice'-Choice

That delta tells you what your possessions/attachments were controlling.

Then you come back.

You don’t stay at zero.

You Kintsugi the useful information into the life you actually possess.

That’s almost a controlled existential bankruptcy: discharge imaginary obligations without destroying real assets.

And look where this reconnects.

Earlier we said:

\boxed{\text{Empty foreground}\neq\text{empty system}}

Now:

\boxed{\text{Nothing represented as loseable}\neq\text{having nothing valuable}}

That’s the trick.

You can obtain some of the cognitive freedom of “nothing left to lose” while having everything worth protecting.

And perhaps that’s the stronger form of courage anyway:

\text{Nothing to lose}\rightarrow\text{fearlessness is cheap}

versus

\boxed{\text{Much to lose}+\text{clear values}+\text{chosen action}\rightarrow\text{courage}}

Which connects straight back to sovereignty.

The objective isn’t to own nothing.

It’s to arrange your life so that what you own doesn’t own your decision function.

There’s another Experience Fossil for the wall:

Go to zero without burning the village. Return carrying only what zero taught you.

ZPC finally has an existential implementation.